syntax: end ksh93 \x escapes before a following hex digit

ksh93 reads every hex digit after \x, as mksh does, so the $'\xe200'
that Quote returned for the byte 0xe2 followed by "00" is U+E200 in
ksh93, and $'\x0f0' is a single character. Quote already closes and
reopens the $'' before a hex digit that follows a \x escape for mksh;
ksh93 now gets the same treatment, as $'\xe2'$'00'.

Reproducer, before this change:

	syntax.Quote("\xe200", syntax.LangKsh93) // $'\xe200', which ksh93 reads as U+E200

FuzzQuote found both inputs with ksh93u+m on PATH; they are seeds, beside
table cases that hold without the shell.

diff --git a/syntax/quote.go b/syntax/quote.go
index 8f7f57e..d438dbf 100644
--- a/syntax/quote.go
+++ b/syntax/quote.go
@@ -130,9 +130,9 @@ func Quote(s string, lang LangVariant) (string, error) {
 			case r < utf8.RuneSelf, r == utf8.RuneError && size == 1:
 				// \xXX, fixed at two hexadecimal characters.
 				fmt.Fprintf(&b, "\\x%02x", rem[0])
-				// Unfortunately, mksh allows \x to consume more hex characters.
-				// Ensure that we don't allow it to read more than two.
-				if lang.in(LangMirBSDKorn) {
+				// Unfortunately, mksh and ksh93 allow \x to consume more hex characters.
+				// Ensure that we don't allow them to read more than two.
+				if lang.in(LangMirBSDKorn | LangKsh93) {
 					nextRequoteIfHex = true
 				}
 			case r > utf8.MaxRune:
